WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.791
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.091 --> 00:00:09.350
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:15.414
Unit nineteen: Year in Review. Today you will tell background stories with the past perfect.

4
00:00:15.714 --> 00:00:18.343
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.556
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:24.708
Retrospective.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:28.657
Retrospective means a look back at events of the past.

8
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.923
The paper published a retrospective in December.

9
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:35.541
Your turn. Say: Retrospective.

10
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.982
Retrospective.

11
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:41.880
Top story.

12
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:45.568
Top story means the most important news item.

13
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:48.171
The flood was the year's top story.

14
00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:51.598
Your turn. Say: Top story.

15
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:55.527
Top story.

16
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:57.168
Timeline.

17
00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:01.181
Timeline means a list of events in the order they happened.

18
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:03.980
A timeline showed each major event.

19
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:07.073
Your turn. Say: Timeline.

20
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:10.934
Timeline.

21
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:12.847
Turning point.

22
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.685
Turning point means a moment when things begin to change.

23
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:19.190
The election was a turning point.

24
00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:22.424
Your turn. Say: Turning point.

25
00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:26.430
Turning point.

26
00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:28.417
Public reaction.

27
00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:32.995
Public reaction means the way ordinary people respond to news.

28
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:35.873
Public reaction was swift and emotional.

29
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:39.890
Your turn. Say: Public reaction.

30
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:43.920
Public reaction.

31
00:01:44.683 --> 00:01:46.369
Long-term impact.

32
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:50.695
Long-term impact means an effect that lasts for many years.

33
00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:53.770
The reform will have a long-term impact.

34
00:01:54.383 --> 00:01:58.057
Your turn. Say: Long-term impact.

35
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.319
Long-term impact.

36
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:04.317
Historic moment.

37
00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:09.011
Historic moment means a time that will be remembered in history.

38
00:02:09.617 --> 00:02:11.616
The signing was a historic moment.

39
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:14.973
Your turn. Say: Historic moment.

40
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:19.206
Historic moment.

41
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.079
Lessons learned.

42
00:02:22.083 --> 00:02:25.395
Lessons learned means useful knowledge gained from experience.

43
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:28.122
Lessons learned help us prepare better.

44
00:02:28.717 --> 00:02:31.624
Your turn. Say: Lessons learned.

45
00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:36.153
Lessons learned.

46
00:02:36.967 --> 00:02:40.699
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:47.058
Visal, this retrospective says 'By December, the city had recovered from the flood.' Why 'had recovered'?

48
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:54.738
The past perfect shows an earlier past event. The recovery happened before December, so the writer adds 'had'.

49
00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:57.455
Is it used in timelines?

50
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:07.588
Yes, for background: 'Before the storm reached land, officials had warned families.' It explains what came first.

51
00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:10.764
And what do year-end reviews look for?

52
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:21.663
A turning point, the public reaction, the long-term impact and lessons learned from each historic moment.

53
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.544
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:28.846
Grammar focus: Past perfect for background events.

55
00:03:29.146 --> 00:03:30.472
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.183 --> 00:03:33.961
By December, the city had recovered from the flood.

57
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.092
Before the storm arrived, officials had warned every family.

58
00:03:38.983 --> 00:03:42.759
When the vote began, many people had already decided who to support.

59
00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:52.308
Use had + past participle for the earlier of two past events. The later event uses the past simple: when the vote began, people had decided.

60
00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:56.561
Be careful! Don't say: When he arrived, we leave had.

61
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.488
Say: When he arrived, we had left.

62
00:04:00.788 --> 00:04:03.832
Use had + past participle for the earlier event.

63
00:04:04.633 --> 00:04:07.123
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:07.933 --> 00:04:08.923
Looking Back.

65
00:04:09.223 --> 00:04:13.823
Every December, newspapers publish a retrospective of the year's top stories.

66
00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:20.165
A timeline shows what had happened before each turning point, such as the flood and the election.

67
00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:27.508
By the end of the year, many families had rebuilt their homes, and public reaction had changed from fear to hope.

68
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.148
Editors ask about the long-term impact of each historic moment.

69
00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:36.329
The lessons learned help readers prepare for the year ahead.

70
00:04:37.433 --> 00:04:41.563
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:42.417 --> 00:04:44.776
By December, the city blank from the flood.

72
00:04:45.076 --> 00:04:49.380
A, had recovered. B, recovers. C, has recover.

73
00:04:52.933 --> 00:04:54.921
The answer is A: had recovered.

74
00:04:56.083 --> 00:04:58.617
A list of events in time order is a blank.

75
00:04:58.917 --> 00:05:03.209
A, timeline. B, retrospective. C, turning point.

76
00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:08.697
The answer is A: timeline.

77
00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.442
A moment when things begin to change is a blank.

78
00:05:12.742 --> 00:05:17.034
A, top story. B, turning point. C, reaction.

79
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.936
The answer is B: turning point.

80
00:05:24.083 --> 00:05:25.851
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:26.151 --> 00:05:36.491
A, Before the storm came, officials warned had.. B, Before the storm came, officials had warned families.. C, Before the storm came, officials has warned..

82
00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:44.448
The answer is B: Before the storm came, officials had warned families..

83
00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:48.883
According to the reading, public reaction changed from fear to…

84
00:05:49.183 --> 00:05:52.651
A, hope. B, anger. C, silence.

85
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.933
The answer is A: hope.

86
00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:02.494
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.882
What do you think will be remembered as this year's most important news story?

88
00:06:15.683 --> 00:06:17.137
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:17.437 --> 00:06:39.449
I think the biggest story will be the recent flood, because it affected so many families. Before it happened, officials had warned people, so the response also showed how important preparation is. Its long-term impact may be better flood defences and stronger communities. I will remember the lessons learned.

90
00:06:39.749 --> 00:06:41.516
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:42.417 --> 00:06:47.356
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past perfect for background events.

92
00:06:47.656 --> 00:06:53.408
Today's value is remembrance. Remember the victims, so that history is never repeated.

93
00:06:53.708 --> 00:06:56.152
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:06:56.452 --> 00:07:00.044
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

